%I A401805 #8 Nov 12 2026 14:21:08
%S A401805 739,907,1579,2851,3607,4153,6073,7789,10663,12637,18451,46807
%N A401805 Primes p congruent to 1 (mod 6) that cannot be written as k*(k+1) + q, where k is a positive integer and q is the smaller member of a twin prime pair.
%C A401805 Blanco-Chacon, McGuire and Robinson (2017) conjecture that every odd prime >= 5 is a sum of a twin prime and twice a triangular number. This sequence lists the exceptions to the variant of that conjecture in which the twin prime must be the smaller member of the pair, restricted to p == 1 (mod 6). (Allowing n = 0 yields the same sequence since no prime p == 1 (mod 6) belongs to A001359; for p == 1 (mod 6) and q == 5 (mod 6), p - q == 2 (mod 6) forces n == 1 (mod 3), reducing candidate density to 1/3.)
%C A401805 All of a(1)..a(12) are representable with the larger twin member as the anchor.
%C A401805 Conjecturally complete: no further terms below 10^9.
%H A401805 Ivan Blanco-Chacon, Gary McGuire, and Oisin Robinson, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.06014">Primes of the form n^2+n+p have density 1</a>, arXiv:1707.06014 [math.NT], 2017.
%e A401805 For p = 739, since 739 - 3 = 736 is not of the form n*(n+1), any lower twin prime q = 739 - n*(n+1) must satisfy q == 5 (mod 6), forcing n == 1 (mod 3). Testing all 9 candidates n in {1, 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25} gives q = 739 - n*(n+1) in {737, 719, 683, 629, 557, 467, 359, 233, 89}, none of which is in A001359, so 739 is in the sequence (whereas 739 = 17*18 + 433 using the larger twin member 433 of (431, 433)).
%o A401805 (PARI) { forprime (p = 7, 10^6, if (p % 6 != 1, next); my(ok = 0, q); for (n = 1, sqrtint(p), q = p - n*(n+1); if (q >= 3 && isprime(q) && isprime(q+2), ok = 1; break)); if (!ok, print1(p, ", "))); }
%Y A401805 Cf. A001359, A002378, A002476.
%K A401805 nonn,more,new
%O A401805 1,1
%A A401805 _Serhat Güven_, Nov 06 2026
